WEBVTT

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:04.100
You put yesterday’s
dinner in the microwave.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:08.350
Two minutes later, the bowl is hot
enough to make you switch hands.

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:09.925
The food? Somehow,

00:00:09.965 --> 00:00:14.080
the middle still tastes like it
came straight from the refrigerator.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.110
So you put it back.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:20.440
Now the edges are furious,
the bowl is even hotter,

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:23.640
and the center is only
slightly less disappointing.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:25.560
Why is that?

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:31.880
There are two questions hiding
in this tiny kitchen argument.

00:00:32.230 --> 00:00:34.510
Why does the food heat unevenly?

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:38.670
And why doesn’t the temperature of the
bowl tell you whether dinner is ready?

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:42.240
Start with what the
microwave actually does.

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:47.332
Inside the appliance, a component
called a magnetron produces microwaves.

00:00:47.372 --> 00:00:50.644
Those waves carry energy
into the cooking chamber,

00:00:50.684 --> 00:00:55.450
where they reflect off the metal
walls and interact with the food.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:59.646
Water molecules respond to
the changing electric field.

00:00:59.686 --> 00:01:02.670
Energy is absorbed and becomes heat.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:07.659
But this isn’t a little oven blowing
an even blanket of hot air over your

00:01:07.699 --> 00:01:08.699
leftovers.

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.880
And it isn’t heating every
part of your dinner equally.

00:01:12.530 --> 00:01:16.330
Imagine an invisible map
inside the cooking chamber.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:21.470
Some regions deliver more microwave
energy. Others deliver less.

00:01:21.720 --> 00:01:24.580
Waves reflecting around
the chamber overlap,

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.170
helping create that uneven pattern.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.920
Our animation makes stronger
regions bright and weaker ones dark.

00:01:32.230 --> 00:01:36.730
Real patterns are more complicated,
and the food itself changes them.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:39.430
But the important idea is simple:

00:01:39.630 --> 00:01:43.110
different places can receive
different amounts of energy.

00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:46.080
That explains why the plate spins.

00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.040
The turntable carries parts of your
meal through different regions,

00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:52.930
helping spread out the
energy they receive.

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:58.840
It improves the situation. It doesn’t
promise identical temperatures everywhere.

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:02.900
Think of people taking turns
standing near a heater.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:05.390
Moving everyone around helps.

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.760
It doesn’t make everyone equally
warm at exactly the same moment.

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:13.060
And dinner has another complication.

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.600
It isn’t one uniform material.

00:02:15.850 --> 00:02:18.620
A thick piece of food, a pool of sauce,

00:02:18.660 --> 00:02:23.080
and a drier section can respond
differently. Their composition, shape,

00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:26.460
and ability to pass heat along all matter.

00:02:26.710 --> 00:02:30.900
That’s why a spinning plate can still
deliver a meal with very different

00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:33.580
temperatures from one bite to the next.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.640
Now for the bowl.

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:41.540
Even microwave-safe containers can become
hot as heat moves into them from the food.

00:02:41.790 --> 00:02:45.410
So a hot bowl can tell you that
heat has reached the container.

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:50.410
It cannot tell you that every part of the
meal has reached the same temperature.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:55.350
The outside of the container and the
center of the food are different places.

00:02:55.600 --> 00:03:00.534
Judging the whole dinner by the rim is
like judging an entire swimming pool by

00:03:00.574 --> 00:03:02.160
touching one corner.

00:03:02.810 --> 00:03:06.460
And there’s one more misconception
making this confusing.

00:03:06.710 --> 00:03:10.810
Microwaves don’t simply cook
thick food from the inside out.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.680
Energy heats regions it can
reach and be absorbed in,

00:03:14.830 --> 00:03:18.120
while heat also spreads
through the food by conduction.

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.700
The middle of a thick portion
may still be catching up.

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:26.614
Adding more time can make already-hot
regions hotter while that process

00:03:26.654 --> 00:03:27.654
continues.

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:29.630
So what helps?

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.740
When the instructions
call for stirring, stir.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:36.100
You’re moving warmer and
cooler portions together,

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.240
rather than leaving the
same cold center in place.

00:03:39.490 --> 00:03:43.430
Follow the recommended covering
and standing instructions, too.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:48.950
Standing time gives heat more opportunity
to spread after the microwave stops.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.460
Follow the food’s package instructions
and your appliance manual,

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:56.410
including any temperature
checks they require.

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.950
The bowl feeling hot isn’t a
substitute for those instructions.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.810
So your microwave doesn’t have a
personal problem with leftovers.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.610
Heating depends on where the
energy goes, what it meets,

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:11.670
and how heat moves afterward.

00:04:11.920 --> 00:04:14.450
The spinning plate helps with location.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:16.810
Stirring moves the food around.

00:04:17.060 --> 00:04:19.270
Standing time helps heat spread.

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:21.110
And that hot bowl?

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:23.020
It’s reporting on the bowl.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:26.150
It isn’t giving you a
complete report on dinner.

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:30.080
Everyday things. Surprising explanations.

00:04:30.330 --> 00:04:31.850
That’s Why Is That?
